Also per the task: "Provide #print axioms plus a fail-closed programmatic axiom audit for every new declaration". The standard approach: a module that lists all declarations of the D12 modules and checks axiom dependencies programmatically. In Lean, `#print axioms decl` shows axioms. A programmatic audit can be done via a script (bash + grep on `#print axioms` output), fail-closed: any axiom outside the allowed set → failure; unknown/empty output → failure. Let me design:

AxiomAudit.lean: a file containing a bunch of `#check` statements referencing every new declaration plus, at the end, marker comments. Then a bash script runs `lake env lean AxiomAudit.lean` — hmm, `#print axioms X` prints for each X. Actually simpler and more robust: bash script extracts all top-level declaration names from the D12 files (parse `theorem`/`def`/`abbrev`/`noncomputable def`/`instance` lines — need care with `where` structures) and then for each, runs `lake env lean` on a generated file with `#print axioms <name>` — that's expensive (hundreds of invocations). Better: generate one audit file with all `#print axioms` commands and parse output lines "depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]".

Fail-closed criteria: every declaration must appear with a nonempty "depends on axioms: [...]" line, and the axiom list must be ⊆ allowed set. Any parse failure (declaration not found, output format mismatch) → failure. Plus negative control: include a known declaration with extra axioms? Hmm, there's no guarantee such exists. Negative control could be: include an intentionally invalid name — expect the audit to catch it? No — negative control: the script should verify it catches a violation by injecting a fake declaration that uses an axiom... In a separate throwaway file (not part of release), define `axiom fake`, a theorem depending on it, and check the auditor flags it. The audit script itself is the fail-closed program. Let me do that in tools/.

